schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Review boundary (consequence-based content tiering)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A written rule sorting knowledge-commons content into tiers by how much
    damage a wrong or misleading entry does if unchallenged (ephemeral/
    personal → working/shared → canonical/load-bearing), with the human
    review gate applied only where consequence is high.
  source_traditions:
    - SuperBenefit Knowledge Garden's raw-notes/working-docs/refined-artifacts tiering
    - Elinor Ostrom's commons-governance design principles (clear boundaries, locally-made rules)
    - Git/pull-request code-review practice
  toolkit_usage: >-
    The organizing idea of human-review-boundaries.md — proposed as the
    mechanism that lets a knowledge commons stay both open to contribution
    and trustworthy for load-bearing claims.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - review-and-maturity-k1-state-model
    - source-system-stewardship
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Sort by "the claim the content makes," not by length or authorship — a
      two-line recorded decision is load-bearing (canonical); a forty-page
      brainstorm is ephemeral.
    - >-
      A review boundary must be legible (written down, findable by a
      stranger) — an unwritten habit in one steward's head is not a boundary.
  tensions:
    - >-
      Cheap-to-pass reviewer checklists vs. the risk that a shrunk review
      becomes a rubber stamp.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Framing this as a settled three-tier model risks obscuring that the
    right number and definition of tiers is genuinely team-specific — the
    article itself says the first boundary drawn "will be wrong" and must be
    revisited.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/human-review-boundaries.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
